<!-- SECTION 6: FINDINGS -->
<div id="findings" style="background: #f5f7fa; padding: 0 0 8px;">
<div class="container">
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">Section 06</span>
      <span class="section-title">Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</span>
    </div>
    <p class="section-desc">Synthesis of the six most actionable insights from this analysis, targeted at energy policy practitioners designing subsidy programs, fuel tax regimes, and price stabilization mechanisms.</p>

    <div class="findings-list">
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">1</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <span class="finding-tag tag-finding">Core Finding</span>
          <h4>Retail fuel prices are almost entirely determined by global crude movements</h4>
          <p>The week-over-week % change in Brent Crude explains 99.6% of global average retail petrol price variance (r = 0.996). For low-subsidy countries — the majority of the dataset — crude-to-retail pass-through is near-instantaneous. Policy interventions that target retail prices without addressing crude market exposure (e.g., temporary price caps) face severe fiscal pressure during commodity upswings.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">2</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <span class="finding-tag tag-policy">Policy Lever</span>
          <h4>Subsidies provide powerful but fiscally costly price insulation</h4>
          <p>High-subsidy countries paid 79.5% less for petrol than Low-subsidy equivalents over the period ($0.71 vs $3.45/L). Very High-subsidy nations (Venezuela, Iran, Libya, Iraq, Algeria, Kuwait, Nigeria, Qatar, Saudi Arabia) paid just $0.15/L — 95.7% below market. However, this insulation comes at extreme fiscal cost, particularly during high-Brent environments (2022–2026 avg $119–124/bbl). The Brent–retail correlation in High-subsidy countries falls to 0.741, confirming genuine insulation, but subsidy expenditures scale non-linearly with crude price.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">3</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <span class="finding-tag tag-finding">Core Finding</span>
          <h4>The COVID-19 shock revealed structural asymmetry in price transmission</h4>
          <p>Global Brent fell from ~$66/bbl (Jan 2020) to $47.97/bbl (Apr 2020) — a 27% drop — yet average retail petrol fell only from ~$2.28/L to ~$1.48/L, a 35% decline partly reflecting lagged pass-through and sticky tax floors. The 2021–2022 recovery phase saw Brent rebound 145% from trough to peak ($130/bbl), with retail prices responding proportionally — confirming that downside relief is shared, but upside shocks are fully transmitted in low-subsidy markets.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">4</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <span class="finding-tag tag-policy">Policy Lever</span>
          <h4>High-income nations use taxation, not subsidies, as their primary fiscal tool — at 2.5× the rate</h4>
          <p>High-income countries average a 34.7% fuel tax rate versus 21.1% in Middle-income and 22.9% in Low-income nations. Low-subsidy countries apply 37.5% tax rates on average, compared to 15% in High-subsidy nations. This reveals a structural trade-off: wealthy nations use fuel taxes for revenue and demand management (accepting high retail prices), while developing nations rely on subsidies for consumer protection (accepting fiscal burden). Middle-income nations are caught between both pressures.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">5</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <span class="finding-tag tag-finding">Core Finding</span>
          <h4>Regional pricing inequality has widened materially since 2020</h4>
          <p>The spread between Europe (highest avg regional petrol) and Middle East (lowest) grew from $1.61/L in 2020 to $2.87/L in 2026 — a 78% widening. Europe's proximity to Russian supply disruptions and its low-subsidy, high-tax structure made it the most exposed region to the 2022 energy crisis (+78% price rise 2020→2022). Meanwhile, Middle East nations absorbed the same crude shock with a price rise of only $0.55/L (+69% in absolute terms but from a much lower base). This divergence underscores how policy design, not geography, is the primary determinant of consumer exposure.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">6</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <span class="finding-tag tag-risk">Risk / Recommendation</span>
          <h4>Sustained high crude prices (2023–2026) make current subsidy architectures financially unsustainable</h4>
          <p>With Brent averaging $104–124/bbl since 2023, the implicit fiscal cost of Very High-subsidy regimes is at historical highs. Nations in this tier (many Low and Middle-income) face a compounding fiscal burden: crude prices are elevated, subsidy expenditure scales directly with the gap between market price and administered price, and exchange rate pressures amplify USD-denominated import costs. Policymakers should consider transitioning from blanket price subsidies to targeted cash-transfer schemes, which deliver equivalent consumer protection at a fraction of the fiscal cost and allow energy prices to send efficient market signals.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="background: white; border-radius: 10px; padding: 28px; margin-top: 28px; border: 1px solid #e8ecf0; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);">
